WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.300
- [Narrator] COVID-19 has
killed more than 300,000 people

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.570
in the United States in 2020.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.530
But that's just the known death toll.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.840
Scientists and families who
have lost their relatives

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.170
across the country, say the real figure

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.747
is likely much higher.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:16.507
- That the total number of
COVID deaths that we have

00:00:16.507 --> 00:00:18.510
in the US is really an undercount

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.330
of the full burden of the pandemic.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.360
- [Narrator] When the CDC
looked at how many more people

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.570
had died nationwide this year
compared to previous years,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.420
it found that COVID-19
only accounted for around

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.200
two thirds of those deaths.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.760
Meaning up to 100,000 additional deaths

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.470
hadn't been officially
attributed to the pandemic.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.010
The number of additional deaths
compared to previous years

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.960
is known as excess deaths

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.990
and many experts see
it as a starting point

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.650
to determine the real death
toll from the pandemic.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.650
- And so when we look at
2020 and the COVID deaths

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.990
there's clearly an excess mortality.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:53.823
And then the question is,

00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:56.540
what is that excess mortality made up of?

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.480
- [Narrator] That's the
puzzle that statisticians,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.140
healthcare workers and
policy makers are all racing

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.180
to solve and it may take years.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.210
- You know, these things
are not as black and white

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.860
as you might expect.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.820
- [Narrator] But understanding
exactly how people die

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.470
can help governments fight
the virus more effectively

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.630
and determine things such as
where to send vaccines first

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.900
and whether lockdowns are effective.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.670
So how will we know how many people

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.833
really died from COVID in America?

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.310
- I have today declared
that the Corona virus

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.610
presents a public health
emergency in the United States.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.000
- [Narrator] When the pandemic
first hit the US in February

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.130
epidemiologists like Dan Weinberger

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.810
from Yale Public School
of Health, began comparing

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.850
how many excess deaths they were seeing

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.530
against the official COVID death figures.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.050
- Especially early on, there
was a pretty considerable gap

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.670
between the number of
reported COVID deaths

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.890
and the total number of
deaths that occurred.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.370
- [Narrator] Professor
Weinberger believes that this gap

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.040
which you can see here is
caused by two main factors.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.810
The first is a term
called collateral deaths

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.710
where people have been
killed not by the COVID virus

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.770
but have died due to the
wider impacts of the pandemic.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.150
- In Michigan, six hospitals

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.840
have reached capacity this week.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.250
- As hospitals are getting

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.620
more and more overwhelmed by the pandemic.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.650
- Simply the ability to care
for people as well as we should

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:15.710
simply goes away.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.130
- [Narrator] In some cases,
researchers say people have died

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.680
because healthcare
facilities were overwhelmed.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.760
Or people simply wanted
to avoid hospitals.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.110
- We know that especially
early on in the pandemic

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.140
a lot of people were
avoiding emergency care

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.990
for things like heart attacks and strokes.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.470
And that can certainly contribute

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.620
to the number of people who are dying.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.490
- [Narrator] The second
category is COVID deaths

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.800
that have been certified
as something else.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.450
And professor Weinberger
believes it's this category

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.990
that makes up the vast
bulk of those non COVID

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.120
excess deaths.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.930
- Determining the cause of
death for a death certificate

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.460
is a bit of an art.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.540
There are often multiple
contributing factors.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.650
So you can imagine that someone
gets an infection with COVID

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.860
and then they develop pneumonia,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.740
perhaps have heart attack and die.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.660
And then there's a question
of what gets recorded

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.110
as the underlying cause of
death on the death certificate?

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.900
- [Narrator] One person
who makes those decisions

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.340
is Dr. James Gill, Connecticut's
Chief Medical Examiner.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.200
He has made it his mission
to find COVID deaths

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.830
that had been misclassified in his state.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.950
- We don't want to be
just arbitrary and say,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.990
well if they've ever been COVID positive,

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.080
we're gonna make it a COVID death, no!

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.960
You really have to look
at the circumstances

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.840
and the clinical history
and make sure that COVID-19

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.830
is still playing or played
some kind of pathologic role

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:35.800
in the death.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.480
- [Narrator] Dr. Gill
started testing bodies

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.500
at funeral homes after seeing
a slew of death certificates

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.900
at the beginning of the
pandemic with pneumonia

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.610
or respiratory failure
listed as the cause.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.040
- If it said respiratory
failure, that would give us

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.880
a tip off that maybe
this was a COVID death

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:53.890
that had been missed.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:57.030
- [Narrator] His team
performed some 175 postmortem

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.720
COVID-19 swabs at funeral
homes in his state.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.740
- 110 tested positive for COVID.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.400
We were able to identify dozens of deaths

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.060
that would not have been
identified as COVID death,

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.350
but for our investigation.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:17.150
- [Narrator] Dr. Gill
believes many COVID deaths

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.250
may have been missed due to a
shortage of available testing

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.370
for both the dead and the living.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.270
Testing kits have become easier to find

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.950
after shortages in May.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.650
- We had trouble getting
enough nasopharyngeal swabs.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.220
To test I was calling up
local hospitals saying,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.463
can you give me 20, can you give me 25?

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.200
They just weren't available.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.900
Unfortunately again, people
think of the medical examiners

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.170
you're just dealing with dead people.

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:42.650
You don't need to be a priority.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.290
We need to deal with the living.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.850
And I absolutely understand
that and agree with that

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.720
to a certain point, but as I, you know

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.370
but our work does affect
the living as well.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.540
- [Narrator] So how does
understanding how people have died

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.230
help us exactly?

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.720
- The disruptions caused by the pandemic

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.440
are obviously unprecedented
in recent history.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.260
Having a better
understanding of the severity

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:08.920
of the pandemic can allow us
to make more informed choices

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:12.100
and can allow the public to decide whether

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.440
those decisions are justified or not.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.960
- [Narrator] The excess deaths
data can also give insights

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.480
into how vaccines should be rolled out.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.770
- You know, the excess
deaths particularly hit

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.610
certain vulnerable
populations especially hard,

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:30.340
certain lower income populations
and communities of color.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.930
It can help in sort of
planning for the next phase

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.810
of the pandemic thinking about

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.420
who should be vaccinated
first and where we might wanna

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.339
focus prevention efforts.

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:41.050
(people clapping)

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.360
- [Narrator] Is there limited resources

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.710
to fight the pandemic?

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.130
This data is crucial to help
identify high priority areas

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.230
and inform policy decisions.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.290
- In addition, people just
wanna know, a family member

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.190
you wanna know if your brother
dies, your father dies,

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.440
why did they die?

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.860
You know, is this a COVID death?

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.850
And so, you know, I hate
to use the term closure

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.960
but that is something
that I think helps people

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.993
with their bereavement and
so they can understand.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.230
- [Narrator] But for those seeking closure

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.570
the answers may still take time.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.330
While professor Weinberger
says data from this pandemic

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.350
is still being analyzed
at an unprecedented rate,

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.940
it's still typically takes years

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.460
to account for those
unknown excess deaths.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.970
- Yeah, I think it is gonna take sometime

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.630
with the current pandemic,

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.450
just because we're
gonna have to be patient

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.830
and wait for the data to be released.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.783
And that could take another
one to two years from now.

